                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* C1 \u2013 the carbon that originally bears the halogen (and the **\u00b9\u00b3C** label).  \n* C2 \u2013 ortho\u2011carbon on the \u201cleft\u201d side of C1.  \n* C6 \u2013 ortho\u2011carbon on the \u201cright\u201d side of C1.  \n\nThe reaction is carried out with a huge excess of\u202f\\( \\ce{NaNH2} \\) in liquid NH\u2083, i.e. under strongly basic conditions.  \nOnly the kinetic information given in the problem statement is used.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (1)   Overall distribution of the C\u2013N bond\n\n| Step | Relative speed | Normalised probability |\n|------|----------------|------------------------|\n| Elimination of H from C2 \u2192 C1\u2011C2 benzyne | 3\u202f\u00d7\u202f(H from C6) | \\(p_{2}=3/(3+1)=\\frac34\\) |\n| Elimination of H from C6 \u2192 C1\u2011C6 benzyne | 1\u202f\u00d7\u202f(H from C2) | \\(p_{6}=1/4\\) |\n\nAfter each benzyne is formed the nucleophile attacks with the following ratios  \n\n* C1\u2011C2 benzyne\u2003\u2192\u2003attack at C1 : C2 = 2\u202f:\u202f3 \u21d2 \\(f_{1|2}=2/5,\\; f_{2|2}=3/5\\)  \n* C1\u2011C6 benzyne\u2003\u2192\u2003attack at C1 : C6 = 1\u202f:\u202f4 \u21d2 \\(f_{1|6}=1/5,\\; f_{6|6}=4/5\\)\n\nOverall fractions (multiply the pathway probability by the intra\u2011pathway fraction and add):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{C1}&=\\underbrace{\\frac34}_{\\text{C1\u2011C2}} \\times \\frac25\n          +\\underbrace{\\frac14}_{\\text{C1\u2011C6}} \\times \\frac15\n        =\\frac{6}{20}+\\frac{1}{20}= \\boxed{\\frac{7}{20}=0.35}\\\\[4pt]\n\\text{C2}&=\\frac34\\times\\frac35 =\\boxed{\\frac{9}{20}=0.45}\\\\[4pt]\n\\text{C6}&=\\frac14\\times\\frac45 =\\boxed{\\frac{4}{20}=0.20}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nCheck: \\(7/20+9/20+4/20=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (2)   Position of the **\u00b9\u00b3C** label\n\nThe **\u00b9\u00b3C** stays on the original C1 carbon throughout the whole sequence.\n\n* **Label directly bonded to NH\u2082** \u2013 this happens only when the nucleophile attacks C1.  \n  Fraction = fraction of product with C\u2013N bond at C1 = **7\u204420 (35\u202f%)**.\n\n* **Label ortho to NH\u2082** \u2013 this occurs whenever the NH\u2082 ends up on C2 or C6, leaving C1 next to the newly attached nitrogen.  \n  Fraction = 1\u202f\u2212\u202f7\u204420 = **13\u204420 (65\u202f%)**.\n\nNo carbon atom moves during the reaction; the apparent \u201cmigration\u2019\u2019 of the label from ipso to ortho simply reflects that the nucleophile can attack either terminus of the benzyne. When it attacks the opposite terminus, the original C1 carbon remains unchanged but becomes ortho to the newly created C\u2013N bond.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (3)   Experiment\u202fB (classical SNAr)\n\nSubstrate: \\([\\!^{13}\\!C]\\)\u20111\u2011fluoro\u20112,4\u2011dinitrobenzene \u2192 attack of \\(\\ce{NH3}\\) proceeds through a Meisenheimer (\u03c3\u2011complex) intermediate.  \nThe nucleophile adds to the **ipso** carbon (the one that bore the fluorine) and the fluorine leaves. Consequently the **\u00b9\u00b3C** atom ends up **directly bonded to the amine** in the product (an aniline derivative).  \n\n*Experiment\u202fA*: the label can be ipso **or** ortho depending on which terminus of the benzyne is attacked.  \n*Experiment\u202fB*: the label is **always ipso**, because the addition\u2011elimination pathway does not allow the nucleophile to attack an ortho position.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (4)   Ranking of 1\u2011halo\u20112,4\u2011dinitrobenzenes when addition is rate\u2011determining\n\nWhen nucleophilic addition (formation of the Meisenheimer complex) is the slow step, the decisive factor is how well the intermediate can stabilise the negative charge.  \nThe halogen contributes an **inductive (\u2011I)** effect that withdraws electron density from the ring, thereby stabilising the anion. Fluorine is the most electronegative halogen, giving the strongest \u2011I effect; chlorine is next, bromine the weakest.\n\nHence the relative rates are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{F} \\;>\\; \\text{Cl} \\;>\\; \\text{Br}}\n\\]\n\nEven though fluoride is a poorer leaving group than chloride or bromide, its superior \u2011I stabilization of the \u03c3\u2011complex outweighs the leaving\u2011group advantage when addition, not departure, controls the rate. This explains why the order can be opposite to the usual SN1/SN2 trend (where the leaving\u2011group ability dominates).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (5)   Why unsubstituted chlorobenzene resists SNAr but reacts with NaNH\u2082 / NH\u2083\n\n* **Addition\u2011elimination (SNAr) pathway**  \n  \u2022 The Meisenheimer intermediate places a negative charge on the aromatic ring adjacent to the chlorine.  \n  \u2022 In unsubstituted chlorobenzene there are **no electron\u2011withdrawing groups** to delocalise that charge, so the intermediate is extremely unstable. The addition step therefore has a very high activation barrier and the reaction is essentially non\u2011reactive under mild conditions.\n\n* **Elimination\u2011addition (benzyne) pathway**  \n  \u2022 NaNH\u2082 is a **very strong base** (pK_a of NH\u2083 \u2248\u202f33, pK_a of NaNH\u2082 \u2248\u202f38). It can abstract the ortho\u2011hydrogen of chlorobenzene, expelling chloride and generating a **benzyne** (a highly strained alkyne).  \n  \u2022 Benzyne does **not** require a Meisenheimer intermediate; it is electrophilic enough to be trapped directly by nucleophiles such as NH\u2082. Hence the same substrate undergoes rapid amination under these strongly basic conditions.\n\nThus electron\u2011withdrawing substituents (as in 2,4\u2011dinitrobenzene) enable the SNAr route, whereas a powerful base enables the alternative benzyne route.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (6)   Interpretation of partial scrambling of the **\u00b9\u00b3C** label\n\nObservation: the **\u00b9\u00b3C** atom appears both **ipso** (attached to NH\u2082) and **ortho** to NH\u2082 in the isolated mixture, but never disappears from the aromatic ring.\n\n* **Consistent mechanism(s)**  \n  \u2013 **Pure SNAr** would give **only ipso** labeling, because the nucleophile adds exclusively to the carbon that originally bore the halogen.  \n  \u2013 **Pure benzyne\u2011addition** (experiment\u202fA) predicts a mixture of ipso and ortho labeling exactly as described (\u2248\u202f35\u202f% ipso, 65\u202f% ortho according to the kinetic model).  \n\nTherefore the observed scrambling is **compatible with the benzyne\u2011addition pathway** (or with a **mixture** of both pathways). It **cannot** be explained by a simple SNAr mechanism alone.\n\n* **What can be inferred**  \n  \u2013 At least one pathway that allows nucleophilic attack at an ortho carbon must be operative.  \n  \u2013 The label itself does **not migrate**; the different positions arise from attack on different termini of the benzyne intermediate.\n\n* **What cannot be concluded**  \n  \u2013 From the isotope pattern alone we cannot decide whether the reaction proceeds **exclusively** via benzyne formation or via a **minor contribution** of the classic SNAr route. Additional evidence (kinetics, temperature dependence, competition with a poor nucleophile, etc.) would be required to quantify the relative contributions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Summary table\n\n| Question | Result |\n|----------|--------|\n| (1) Fractions of C\u2013N bond location | C1\u202f=\u202f7\u204420 (35\u202f%), C2\u202f=\u202f9\u204420 (45\u202f%), C6\u202f=\u202f4\u204420 (20\u202f%) |\n| (2) **\u00b9\u00b3C** position | Directly bound to NH\u2082: 7\u204420; Ortho to NH\u2082: 13\u204420. No carbon migration needed. |\n| (3) Experiment\u202fB label | **\u00b9\u00b3C** ends up **ipso** (bonded to NH\u2082); contrasts with A where it can be ipso **or** ortho. |\n| (4) Rate order (addition RDS) | **F\u202f>\u202fCl\u202f>\u202fBr** \u2013 strong \u2013I effect outweighs leaving\u2011group ability. |\n| (5) Unsubstituted chlorobenzene | Resistant to SNAr (no EW groups to stabilise Meisenheimer complex); reacts with NaNH\u2082 because the strong base generates benzyne, bypassing the Meisenheimer step. |\n| (6) Scrambling observation | Consistent with benzyne\u2011addition (or a mixture of pathways); excludes a pure SNAr mechanism. |\n\nThese answers together illustrate how kinetic isotope effects and mechanistic considerations differentiate the two possible routes\u2014elimination\u2011addition via benzyne and addition\u2011elimination via a Meisenheimer intermediate\u2014in aromatic nucleophilic substitution.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multiple subquestions.\n\nFirst, define fractions.\n\nLet\u2019s denote rates: elimination at C2 vs C6. Let k2 = 3k6 (three times faster). So probability of forming C1-C2 benzyne versus C1-C6 benzyne proportional to k2 * [H+] etc. Since only one proton eliminated per molecule, we consider relative probabilities: p2 = 3, p6 = 1. Normalize: total weight = 4. So fraction forming C1-C2 benzyne = 3/4, C1-C6 benzyne = 1/4.\n\nNow after each benzyne forms, nucleophilic attack distribution:\n\nFor C1-C2 benzyne: attack at C1 : C2 = 2:3. Total parts =5. So fraction of that pathway leading to C1 = 2/5, to C2 =3/5.\n\nSimilarly for C1-C6 benzyne: attack at C1:C6 =1:4 => fractions 1/5 to C1, 4/5 to C6.\n\nNow overall fractions of product where new C-N bond formed at each carbon.\n\nCompute overall probability for C1: from pathway C1-C2 (prob 3/4) * 2/5 + from pathway C1-C6 (1/4)*1/5 = (3/4)*(2/5)+(1/4)*(1/5)= (6/20)+(1/20)=7/20=0.35.\n\nC2: only from first pathway: (3/4)*(3/5)=9/20=0.45.\n\nC6: only from second pathway: (1/4)*(4/5)=4/20=0.20.\n\nCheck sum: 7+9+4 =20/20 =1 good.\n\nThus fractions: C1 35%, C2 45%, C6 20%.\n\nNow part (2): ^13C label stays on original C1 carbon. After reaction, the C1 carbon may end up being part of C-N bond if attack occurs at C1, otherwise it will be ortho to NH2 when attack occurs at C2 or C6 because those carbons become attached to NH2 while C1 remains adjacent.\n\nSo fraction of product where ^13C is directly bonded to NH2 equals fraction where attack at C1 = 7/20 = 0.35.\n\nFraction where ^13C is ortho to NH2 equals when NH2 attaches elsewhere (C2 or C6) and C1 is now ortho to that nitrogen. That is 1 - 0.35 = 13/20 = 0.65. Indeed.\n\nExplain why no migration needed: Because benzyne formation eliminates H from ortho carbon, creating triple bond between C1 and eliminated carbon (say C2). In the resulting benzyne, the original C1 retains its identity; nucleophile can attack either terminus. When attack occurs at the other terminus (C2 or C6), the carbon bearing the label (C1) ends up adjacent to the newly attached NH2, giving ortho relationship without moving the label.\n\nNow experiment B: Substrate is [1-13C]-1-fluoro-2,4-dinitrobenzene. Reaction via SNAr addition-elimination. Attack of NH3 occurs at C1 (the ipso position) to give amine at same carbon. No rearrangement. So ^13C label ends up directly bonded to NH2 (ipso). Contrast: In experiment A, label can be ipso (when attack at C1) or ortho (when attack at C2 or C6). In B it's always ipso.\n\nNow (4): Ranking F > Cl > Br? Need to think about addition-elimination SNAr where nucleophilic addition (formation of Meisenheimer complex) is rate-determining. Electron withdrawing groups stabilize the negative charge in the intermediate. Leaving group ability influences step after addition (departure). If addition is RDS, better leaving group may not matter much; but still leaving group influences stability of intermediate? Actually after addition, the intermediate is a sigma-complex where the leaving group is still attached; departure occurs later (fast?). If addition is RDS, then the rate depends mainly on ability to stabilize the negative charge, which is independent of leaving group. However leaving group may affect the energy barrier for addition because stronger leaving groups may lower activation energy due to easier departure? Typically for SNAr, rate order: F < Cl < Br < I (more reactive halogen gives faster reaction) because better leaving groups accelerate overall reaction. But if addition is RDS, leaving group effect should be minimal; however there is some effect because the transition state resembles the intermediate with the leaving group partially departed; better leaving groups lower TS energy. Thus ranking: Br > Cl > F (faster with better LG). But they ask \"assuming nucleophilic addition is rate determining\" rank substrates fastest to slowest. Usually for SNAr, fluorine is most reactive despite being poor LG because strong -I effect stabilizes Meisenheimer complex. Wait typical reactivity: 1-fluoro-2,4-dinitrobenzene reacts fastest among halogens in SNAr. Indeed F is best due to strong inductive effect outweighing leaving group ability. So if addition is RDS, the -I effect dominates, making F fastest, then Cl, then Br (least). So ranking fastest to slowest: F > Cl > Br.\n\nNeed explanation: With addition RDS, the key factor is stabilization of the anionic sigma complex; electronegative halogen provides greater -I stabilization than Cl or Br. Leaving-group ability matters less; thus contrary to SN1/SN2 where LG ability dominates.\n\nNow (5): Why unsubstituted chlorobenzene resists SNAr but can undergo amination with NaNH2 in liquid NH3. Answer: Without electron-withdrawing substituents, the Meisenheimer intermediate would place a negative charge on aromatic ring adjacent to chlorine, which is highly destabilized; therefore addition step has high barrier, so SNAr slow. However NaNH2 is a very strong base (pKa of NH3 ~33, NaNH2 pKa ~38?), it can abstract a proton from the ortho position to generate benzyne (elimination-addition pathway). Benzyne formation bypasses the need for a Meisenheimer complex; the benzyne intermediate is a strained alkyne that readily reacts with nucleophile. Thus the strong base facilitates elimination of HCl to give benzyne, which then undergoes nucleophilic addition to give anilines. Electron-withdrawing groups like nitro in dinitrobenzene stabilize the Meisenheimer complex, lowering barrier for addition-elimination SNAr, making that pathway viable even without strong base.\n\nNow (6): Observation of partial scrambling of ^13C label between ipso and ortho positions relative to NH2, but no loss from ring. This suggests that both pathways (benzyne addition-elimination and SNAr) could occur, or perhaps interconversion of benzyne intermediates leads to scrambling. However the model says benzynes don't interconvert. Partial scrambling indicates that some product formed via SNAr (label stays ipso) and some via benzyne (label can be ortho). Therefore observation is consistent with mixture of mechanisms, but cannot definitively assign which pathway predominates without additional data (e.g., kinetics, stereochemistry). It also cannot prove that label migrates; rather it reflects different sites of nucleophilic attack.\n\nThus answer: mixture of both mechanisms plausible; can't rule out either solely based on isotope pattern; but presence of ortho labeling rules out pure SNAr (which would give only ipso). However partial scrambling could arise from competing benzyne pathway; cannot infer whether benzyne interconverts or whether there is exchange after formation.\n\nNow craft final answer with clear sections."
